<section class="page-head"><div class="container"><div class="eyebrow">Backend · FastAPI · PostgreSQL · Stripe</div><h1>FlyRank Usage Metering & Billing Engine</h1><p class="section-lead">A backend capstone for tenant-scoped API and AI-token metering, plan quotas, Stripe test-mode subscription synchronization, cost estimation and monthly usage reconciliation.</p><div class="actions"><a class="cta" href="https://flyrank-capstone-metering-billing-ai.onrender.com/demo" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open live demo →</a><a class="cta secondary" href="https://github.com/NiknaxTheGreek/flyrank-capstone-metering-billing-ai" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">View GitHub repository →</a></div></div></section>

<section><div class="container content">
<h2>Problem</h2>
<p>A multi-tenant AI product needs more than a usage counter. It needs to know which tenant consumed what, prevent accidental double counting, enforce plan limits consistently, synchronize paid entitlements safely, and make usage auditable enough to reconcile later.</p>

<h2>System design</h2>
<ul>
<li>FastAPI service with SQLAlchemy-backed persistence and PostgreSQL as the target database.</li>
<li>Two seeded plans: Free at 1,000 API calls and 100,000 AI tokens per UTC month; Pro at 10,000 calls and 1,000,000 tokens.</li>
<li>Tenant-bound HMAC proofs on tenant-scoped public endpoints.</li>
<li>Exactly-once usage recording through a database uniqueness constraint on tenant plus idempotency key.</li>
<li>Independent API-call and AI-token quota enforcement with exact-limit requests accepted.</li>
<li>Current-period usage summaries with remaining allowance and integer-cent AI cost estimates.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Stripe entitlement flow</h2>
<p>Checkout runs in Stripe test mode. Creating a Checkout Session does not grant Pro access locally. Entitlements change only after verified subscription webhooks are received and processed.</p>
<ul>
<li>Raw request bodies are verified against <code>Stripe-Signature</code> before database work.</li>
<li>Supported subscription events link customer/subscription state, grant Pro only for an active subscription containing the configured Pro Price, and restore Free access on deletion.</li>
<li>Stripe event IDs are globally deduplicated so valid webhook retries are idempotent.</li>
<li>Forged, stale, malformed or wrong-secret events are rejected without local state mutation.</li>
</ul>

<div class="callout">A key boundary is deliberate: Checkout initiation is not an entitlement grant. Verified subscription state is the authority.</div>

<h2>Metering & cost estimates</h2>
<p>AI usage is categorized as input, cached input, output or reasoning tokens. Estimated cost uses integer arithmetic and one final half-up rounding step, avoiding floating-point billing drift. The estimate is explicitly not an invoice, tax calculation, charge or overage bill.</p>

<h2>Monthly reconciliation</h2>
<p>A separately runnable rollup job derives each tenant/month summary from the same source-event logic used by the live usage endpoint. Unique tenant-and-period rows make reruns idempotent, and transient database failures retry before exiting nonzero.</p>

<h2>Testing & scope control</h2>
<p>The project includes deterministic tests and a repeatable demo rehearsal that exercises near-quota refusal, idempotent retries, durable usage rows, signed Stripe Free-to-Pro synchronization, forged and duplicate webhook handling, exact usage summaries and a selected pytest result.</p>
<p>The public demo simulates AI usage and uses <strong>Stripe test mode only</strong>. It is hosted on Render's free tier, so after inactivity the service may take about a minute to wake up. Deliberate non-goals include real AI-provider calls, real invoices or collections, production Stripe, production identity, Kubernetes and paid infrastructure.</p>

<h2>What this case proves</h2>
<p>I can design and implement a disciplined backend service where correctness depends on tenancy boundaries, idempotency, quota semantics, signed third-party events, reproducible tests and clear separation between estimates, entitlements and real billing.</p>

